  "reason": "The records collectively provide relevant ingredients: high-temperature treatment of HEAs, element-dependent diffusion toward surfaces (especially R2, R11, R15, and R16), thermally induced segregation or phase instability (R4 and R14), and preferential loss by evaporation during rapid heating (R7). R3 also identifies surface free energy as a potentially important HEA surface driver, while R20 emphasizes kinetic control. However, none describes heating sub-10-nm AuCoCuNiPt particles under vacuum or links Au/Cu surface-energy-driven segregation to nanoparticle reshaping, locally enhanced diffusion, and subsequent evaporation. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but combining these ideas into the later paper's specific intervention and mechanism would require a substantial new step."
